[TEXT] I now have all the evidence I need. Let me consolidate the findings:

- ispa's `dhcpd.conf`: `default-lease-time 600` (10 minutes) — the `max-lease-time` is 7200s, making this 12× shorter than the maximum
- r1's dhclient.leases: shows three consecutive 600-second leases for eth2 (renewals every ~5 minutes)
- r1's default route `K>* 0.0.0.0/0 via 203.0.113.1 dev eth2` is a DHCP-installed kernel route that disappears when the lease expires
- r1's FRR OSPF uses `default-information originate` (without `always`): it withdraws the OSPF default to all internal routers (csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, etc.) whenever that kernel route disappears
- No ispb default route on r1: the single remaining WAN path has zero failover
- No tc/netem, no interface errors — the physical layer is clean

